const d2 = deck({
  part: 2,
  title: 'Rectilinear Figures: Parallelograms — Part 2: Rhombus, rectangle and square',
  goals: ['Describe the rhombus, rectangle and square as special parallelograms', 'Prove that the diagonals of a rhombus meet at right angles', 'Use the diagonal properties of rectangles and squares in problems', 'Identify a figure from the properties of its diagonals'],
  slides: [
    sl('title', 'Rhombus, rectangle and square', { b: 'Add one condition to a parallelogram and you get a **rhombus**, a **rectangle** or a **square**. Each keeps every parallelogram property and gains something about its **diagonals**.', v: flowFamily, n: 'Welcome to part two. Start with a parallelogram. Make all four sides equal and you get a rhombus. Make one angle a right angle and you get a rectangle. Do both and you get a square. Each of these special parallelograms keeps every property from part one, and gains new facts about its diagonals. We prove one and use all of them.' }),
    sl('concept', 'The rhombus', { b: 'A **rhombus** is a parallelogram with **all four sides equal**. It has every property of a parallelogram: opposite angles equal and diagonals that bisect each other.', v: F.figRh(), n: 'A rhombus is a parallelogram with all four sides equal. The tick marks in the figure show the four equal sides, and the arrows show that opposite sides are parallel. A rhombus is a parallelogram first, so its opposite angles are equal, neighbouring angles add to one hundred eighty, and its diagonals bisect each other. The new fact is about the diagonals.' }),
    sl('concept', 'Diagonals of a rhombus', { b: '**Theorem.** The diagonals of a rhombus meet at **right angles**, and each diagonal **bisects** the angles at its ends.', v: F.figRhDiag(), n: 'The diagonals of a rhombus cross at a right angle, marked by the small square. They also bisect each other, shown by the tick marks, and each diagonal bisects the angles at its ends. This makes four congruent right triangles at the centre. In the figure the diagonals are thirty two and twenty four centimetres, so the half lengths are sixteen and twelve, and every side is twenty.' }),
    sl('concept', 'Proof: which triangles to compare', { b: 'Compare $\\triangle AOB$ and $\\triangle AOD$ (shaded): $AB = AD$, $OB = OD$ and $AO$ is common.', v: F.figRhProof(), n: 'To prove that the diagonals are perpendicular, compare the shaded triangles A O B and A O D. The sides A B and A D are equal, because they are sides of a rhombus. O B equals O D, because the diagonals of a parallelogram bisect each other. And A O is common to both. So the three sides match, and the triangles are congruent.' }),
    sl('concept', 'Proof: the angles at O', { b: 'The matching angles at $O$ are equal, and they lie on a straight line.', v: rhProofT, n: 'Here is the full proof. The three pairs of sides are equal, so triangles A O B and A O D are congruent, side side side. Then the angles at O, A O B and A O D, are equal. But they lie on the straight line B D, so they add up to one hundred eighty degrees. Two equal angles with sum one hundred eighty must each be ninety degrees. So the diagonals meet at right angles.' }),
    sl('check', 'Quick check: every rhombus', { b: 'Pick the property that holds for every rhombus.', chk: 'm12', v: F.figRhDiag(), n: 'Which statement holds for every rhombus, including a rhombus that is not a square? Think about the figure. Remember that equal diagonals and right angles at the corners belong only to the square. Look for the property proved on the last slide.' }),
    sl('example', 'Worked example 1: the side from the diagonals', { b: 'The diagonals of a rhombus are $48$ cm and $14$ cm. Find the length of a side and the perimeter.', ul: ["**Step 1 —** $OA = 24$ cm, $OB = 7$ cm, $\\angle AOB = 90^\\circ$.","**Step 2 —** $AB^2 = 24^2 + 7^2 = 625$, so $AB = 25$ cm.","**Answer —** perimeter $= 4 \\times 25 = 100$ cm."], v: F.figRhNum(), n: `The diagonals bisect each other at right angles, so the half lengths are ${nw(24)} and ${nw(7)}, and they form a right triangle with a side of the rhombus as hypotenuse. By Pythagoras, the side squared is ${nw(24 * 24)} plus ${nw(49)}, which is ${nw(625)}, so the side is ${nw(25)} centimetres. The perimeter is four times ${nw(25)}, which is ${nw(100)} centimetres.` }),
    sl('example', 'Worked example 2: angles of a rhombus', { b: 'In rhombus $ABCD$, $\\angle DAB = 76^\\circ$. Find $\\angle ABC$, $x = \\angle BAC$ and $\\angle ABD$.', ul: ["**Step 1 —** $\\angle ABC = 180^\\circ - 76^\\circ = 104^\\circ$.","**Step 2 —** $x = \\dfrac{76^\\circ}{2} = 38^\\circ$ (the diagonal bisects $\\angle A$).","**Answer —** $\\angle ABD = \\dfrac{104^\\circ}{2} = 52^\\circ$."], v: F.figRhAng(), n: `Neighbouring angles add to one hundred eighty, so angle A B C is ${nw(104)} degrees. The diagonal A C bisects the angle at A, so x is half of ${nw(76)}, which is ${nw(38)} degrees. The diagonal B D bisects the angle at B, so angle A B D is half of ${nw(104)}, which is ${nw(52)} degrees. Check: in the right triangle at O, ${nw(38)} plus ${nw(52)} is ninety.` }),
    sl('check', 'Quick check: a rhombus fill-in', { b: 'Find the perimeter from the two diagonals.', chk: 'f3', v: ex1T, n: 'A rhombus shaped brooch has diagonals of ten centimetres and twenty four centimetres. What is its perimeter? Follow the worked example: find half of each diagonal, use the right angle at the centre, then multiply the side by four.' }),
    sl('concept', 'The rectangle', { b: 'A **rectangle** is a parallelogram with **four right angles**. Its diagonals are **equal** and bisect each other, so $OA = OB = OC = OD$.', v: F.figRect(), n: 'A rectangle is a parallelogram with four right angles. Its diagonals are equal, and because they bisect each other, the four pieces from the centre O are all equal. In the figure the four tick marks show that O A, O B, O C and O D are equal. So the centre of a rectangle is the same distance from all four corners. Next, why the diagonals are equal.' }),
    sl('concept', 'Why the diagonals of a rectangle are equal', { b: 'Compare $\\triangle ABC$ and $\\triangle DCB$ on the common side $BC$.', v: reProofT, n: 'Compare the triangles A B C and D C B. A B equals D C, opposite sides. The angles at B and C are both right angles. And B C is common. Two sides and the angle between them agree, so the triangles are congruent, side angle side. So A C equals D B: the diagonals are equal. Notice that the right angle was needed. A general parallelogram does not have equal diagonals.' }),
    sl('example', 'Worked example 3: a rectangle with a diagonal', { b: 'A rectangle has length $24$ cm and diagonal $26$ cm. Find the breadth and the perimeter of $\\triangle AOB$.', ul: ["**Step 1 —** $BC^2 = 26^2 - 24^2 = 100$, so $BC = 10$ cm.","**Step 2 —** $OA = OB = \\dfrac{26}{2} = 13$ cm.","**Answer —** perimeter of $\\triangle AOB = 24 + 13 + 13 = 50$ cm."], v: F.figRectNum(), n: `In the right triangle A B C, the breadth squared is ${nw(26 * 26)} minus ${nw(24 * 24)}, which is ${nw(100)}, so the breadth is ${nw(10)} centimetres. The diagonals are equal and bisect each other, so O A and O B are each half of ${nw(26)}, which is ${nw(13)}. The perimeter of triangle A O B is ${nw(24)} plus ${nw(13)} plus ${nw(13)}, which is ${nw(50)} centimetres.` }),
    sl('example', 'Worked example 4: angles in a rectangle', { b: 'In rectangle $ABCD$, $\\angle ADB = 28^\\circ$. Find $y = \\angle ABD$ and $z = \\angle AOB$.', ul: ["**Step 1 —** $y = 90^\\circ - 28^\\circ = 62^\\circ$.","**Step 2 —** $OA = OB$, so $\\angle OAB = 62^\\circ$.","**Answer —** $z = 180^\\circ - 2 \\times 62^\\circ = 56^\\circ$."], v: F.figRectAng(), n: `The angle at A is a right angle, so in triangle A B D the angle y is ninety minus ${nw(28)}, which is ${nw(62)} degrees. The diagonals are equal and bisect each other, so O A equals O B, and triangle A O B is isosceles with base angles ${nw(62)}. So z is one hundred eighty minus two times ${nw(62)}, which is ${nw(56)} degrees.` }),
    sl('concept', 'The square', { b: 'A **square** is both a rectangle and a rhombus: four equal sides and four right angles. Its diagonals are **equal**, meet at **right angles**, bisect each other, and make **45 degrees** with the sides.', v: F.figSq(), n: 'A square is a rectangle and a rhombus at the same time. It has four equal sides and four right angles. Its diagonals are equal, like a rectangle, and meet at right angles, like a rhombus. They also bisect the corners, so each diagonal makes forty five degrees with the sides. The figure shows the forty five degree angle, the right angle at the centre, and the equal half diagonals.' }),
    sl('check', 'Quick check: a square has both', { b: 'Decide whether a square has the properties of both families.', chk: 'tf4', v: sqT, n: 'A square has every property of a rectangle and every property of a rhombus. True or false? Look at the table. Think about which family gives the equal diagonals, and which family gives the diagonals that meet at right angles.' }),
    sl('example', 'Worked example 5: the area from the diagonal', { b: 'A square has a diagonal of $18$ cm. Find its area.', ul: ["**Step 1 —** The diagonal cuts the square into two triangles of base $18$ cm.","**Step 2 —** Their height is $OA = 9$ cm.","**Answer —** area $= 2 \\times \\dfrac{1}{2} \\times 18 \\times 9 = 162$ cm$^{2}$."], v: F.figSqNum(), n: `The diagonals of a square are equal, meet at right angles and bisect each other. The diagonal B D cuts the square into two triangles. Each has base ${nw(18)} and height O A, which is half of ${nw(18)}, so ${nw(9)}. The area of the square is two times half of ${nw(18)} times ${nw(9)}, which is ${nw(162)} square centimetres.` }),
    sl('concept', 'Comparing the family', { b: 'Read the table by columns: each shape lists its **diagonal** properties.', v: familyT, n: 'This table compares the four shapes. All of them have opposite sides equal and parallel, and diagonals that bisect each other. Equal diagonals belong to the rectangle and the square. Diagonals at right angles belong to the rhombus and the square. Only the square has all six properties. Use this table to decide which facts you may use in a problem.' }),
    sl('concept', 'Identifying the shape from its diagonals', { b: 'In a parallelogram, **perpendicular diagonals** make a rhombus (the proof above gives four equal sides) and **equal diagonals** make a rectangle.', v: identT, n: 'We can also work backwards. In a parallelogram, if the diagonals meet at right angles, the triangles at the centre are congruent, and all four sides are equal, so it is a rhombus. If the diagonals are equal, all four angles are right angles, so it is a rectangle. If both hold, the figure is a square. This is the reverse of what we proved.' }),
    sl('example', 'Worked example 6: a mixed problem', { b: 'The diagonals of a parallelogram $ABCD$ meet at right angles. $AB = 13$ cm and $AC = 24$ cm. Name the figure and find $BD$, the perimeter and the area.', ul: ["**Step 1 —** Perpendicular diagonals: a **rhombus**, so each side is $13$ cm.","**Step 2 —** $OA = 12$, $OB^2 = 13^2 - 12^2 = 25$, so $OB = 5$ and $BD = 10$ cm.","**Answer —** perimeter $52$ cm; area $4 \\times \\dfrac{1}{2} \\times 12 \\times 5 = 120$ cm$^{2}$."], v: F.figMix(), n: `The diagonals are perpendicular, so the parallelogram is a rhombus, with four sides of ${nw(13)} centimetres. O A is half of ${nw(24)}, which is ${nw(12)}. In the right triangle A O B, O B squared is ${nw(169)} minus ${nw(144)}, which is ${nw(25)}, so O B is ${nw(5)} and B D is ${nw(10)} centimetres. The perimeter is ${nw(52)}. The area is four triangles of half times ${nw(12)} times ${nw(5)}, which is ${nw(120)} square centimetres.` }),
    sl('summary', 'Summary of part 2', { b: 'Special parallelograms keep **all** the parallelogram facts and add one more about their diagonals.', v: sum2T, n: 'Here is the summary of part two. The diagonals of a rhombus bisect each other at right angles and bisect the corner angles. The diagonals of a rectangle are equal, so the four half diagonals are equal. A square has all of these facts, and its diagonals make forty five degrees with the sides. Any of them is also a parallelogram, so the diagonals always bisect each other.' }),
    sl('exam-tips', 'Exam tips: how marks are awarded', { ul: ['**Rhombus proof (3 marks):** the matching sides 1 + congruent triangles 1 + straight line, so each angle is $90^\\circ$ 1', '**Lengths:** use the **half-diagonals** as the legs of the right triangle', '**Angles:** name which diagonal bisects which angle, with the reason', '**Mistake:** diagonals of a rhombus equal, or diagonals of a rectangle perpendicular', '**Always** state the family first: rhombus, rectangle or square'], v: tips2T, n: 'Here is how marks are awarded. The rhombus proof earns one mark for the matching sides, one for the congruent triangles, and one for the straight line argument that gives ninety degrees. In length problems, use the half diagonals as the legs of the right triangle. In angle problems, say which diagonal bisects which angle. The common mistakes are mixing up equal and perpendicular diagonals.' }),
  ],
});

